00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.120
Alberta Oil Sands Alliance sets late 2027 target for pathways carbon capture investment decision. This is Environmental Intelligence, episode 65. The Pathways Alliance announced a late 2027 investment decision target for carbon capture in Alberta's oil sands. Record Grizzly Bear Mortalities were recorded in Alberta in 2024, with 40 bears killed by people and 21 of those by law enforcement.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:52.300
Pipeline routing challenges continue in British Columbia's Fraser Valley, where Trans Mountain's prior steep and narrow sections already cost billions. If you only note one thing today for your compliance calendar, Alberta operators now have a fixed late 2027 milestone for pathways carbon capture under the EPA and Federal Industrial Emissions Frameworks.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:01:20.800
The Alliance established a specific late 2027 deadline for the investment decision on the carbon capture network. This moves compliance planning forward for operators under Alberta EPA and Federal Industrial Emissions Frameworks. No immediate filing changes are required, but project schedules must now reference this date. Operators can use the interval to refine measurement, reporting, and verification protocols ahead of the decision.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:34.720
Alberta government data show 40 Grizzlies killed by people in 2024, with 21 by law enforcement. This directly informs SARA habitat protection orders and projects screening in Grizzly range.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:47.060
Consultants should update species at risk assessments for any Alberta sites in affected zones. The fact that more than half the deaths came from law enforcement actions marks a departure from prior years.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:02:04.280
No qualifying peer reviewed or technical reports with direct Canadian regulatory implications appeared today. Trans Mountain's steep, narrow routing experience is informing Alberta's approach to a new west coast pipeline. Yet the current plan contains gaps in terrain risk assessment.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:13.400
Environmental consultants evaluating pipeline corridors in British Columbia must account for these unresolved geotechnical and regulatory constraints.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:26.320
Project timelines and mitigation budgets will likely increase. The earlier routing difficulties already added billions to Trans Mountain costs, highlighting the need for more rigorous pre-construction terrain analysis.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:33.500
Alberta's proposed avoidance measures remain incomplete on key slopes stability and water course crossing details.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:46.760
A client with an active oil sands facility under EPEA asks whether the pathways late 2027 investment decision alters the emissions reduction schedule already embedded in their current approval.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:56.340
The science here is that carbon capture deployment affects both direct stack emissions and the site specific risk assessments used to set permit conditions.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:15.240
Earlier models assumed capture would be operational by 2030, but the new target compresses engineering and permitting windows. In practice, the experienced reviewer notices that output based pricing benchmarks can shift once the investment decision is locked, even before physical construction begins.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:27.340
Field data from similar projects show that pre-decision modeling often underestimates the time required for federal provincial harmonization on measurement, reporting, and verification protocols.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:37.680
Renewal applications must therefore demonstrate how the facility will meet interim intensity targets if capture infrastructure is delayed past the 2027 decision point.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:48.600
The late 2027 milestone also influences assumptions in the site's closure plan, particularly for long-term liability calculations tied to residual emissions.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:04:00.300
Alberta Environment and Parks reviewers have begun requesting sensitivity analyses that test both a 2028 operational start and a 2032 start within the same submission package.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:12.200
The mistake I see most often is treating the investment decision date as a distant planning item rather than a trigger for immediate updates to the site's annual compliance report and closure plan assumptions.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:25.340
The fix is straightforward. Insert the late 2027 milestone into the next EPA renewal submission and rerun the emissions forecast using the current output based allocation factors.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:34.320
If you're working on oil sand's projects in Alberta, review EPA approvals against the late 2027 pathways timeline.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:40.940
Update species at risk screening protocols to reflect 2024 grizzly mortality data in Alberta.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.340
Monitor British Columbia pipeline routing assessments for new geotechnical or environmental constraints.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.140
Brief clients on how the carbon capture decision date affects current emissions reduction commitments.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.300
Track Alberta oil sands carbon capture investment decision progress toward late 2027.

